#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644245 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b564348aaed279585e1025b0e0d4f77a8b84cd659be3e7702ee3590ff9016217.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(5,-5)$ and $(-3,-5)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(5,-5)$ and $Y(-3,-5),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(5-(-3))^2+((-5)-(-5))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{5+(-3)}{2},\\frac{-5+(-5)}{2})=(1,-5)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(1,-5),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-(-5))^{2} & =(4)^2 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644254 :question/problem "![](images/25/25881bcaea3899a61dbbea369276ec417e9ee4f89f3a6b4d4c5082186131eb34.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(8,-2)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(8,-2)$ and $Y(0,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(8-0)^2+((-2)-(-2))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{-2+(-2)}{2})=(4,-2)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(4,-2),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & =(4)^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644267 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a7b210d9f5174075b6fa5c47cbd3857449930116bebe70ba73342e146b95e6e.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-4,5)$ and $(-4,3)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-4,5)$ and $Y(-4,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-4)-(-4))^2+(5-3)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-4+(-4)}{2},\\frac{5+3}{2})=(-4,4)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=1$ and center $(a,b)=(-4,4),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =(1)^2 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644274 :question/problem "![](images/41/41e5667e2c8ac5ea137ea1fc4ccc95853e76bf73623554d65457d566129dd03e.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(2,1)$ and the point $X(7,-6)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(7-2)}^{2}+{(-6-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{74}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{74})^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =74\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644285 :question/problem "![](images/34/342d9e97733b5eb453b703bc38757e5d5a1c94881c28f5d85c3809e3648566ce.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-1,5)$ and the point $X(-6,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-6-(-1))}^{2}+{(-1-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{61}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{61})^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =61\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644292 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd0281b7eaed1b648e725b437b362da1268f3dfaf2e2c324d94c45f8bd9aed77.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-2,-1)$ and the point $X(4,5)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{(4-(-2))^{2}+(5-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(6\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644295 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd37fcb3ef9e8e000ec8da12e8fda150e30cff16cfe96f4a6dec14af725b3a2e.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(4,3)$ and the point $X(6,-4)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-4-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{53}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{53})^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =53\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186644303 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1da4ee77880bb0c8ece506cf794fc30d4a4176f121374d020b46609c3bd01d1.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-2,2)$ and the point $X(1,-3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-2))}^{2}+{(-3-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{34})^{2} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645699 :question/problem "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-9,-1,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -9 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))i-((-9)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)j+((-9)\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =8i+24j+24k \\\\  & =⟨8,24,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{24}^{2}+{24}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+576+576} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1216} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645713 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(9,4,-3),$ $B(4,4,-2),$ $C(5,6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+4j-2k)-(9i+4j-3k) \\\\  & =-5i+k \\\\  & =⟨-5,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+6j-4k)-(9i+4j-3k) \\\\  & =-4i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)i-((-5)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 1)j+((-5)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i-9j-10k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-10)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{185}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{185}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645731 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(1,2,0),$ $B(4,3,1),$ $C(2,5,3).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =3i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+5j+3k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645743 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $BM$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(4,0,1)$, $B(0,3,-1)$, $C(-1,-1,2).$\n\n$BM=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $BM$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $BM,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AB}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i-j+2k)-(4i+k) \\\\  & =-5i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3j-k)-(4i+k) \\\\  & =-4i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3)i-((-5)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-4))j+((-5)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-4))k \\\\  & =1i+14j+19k \\\\  & =⟨1,14,19⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{19}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{558}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AC$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot \\sqrt{27} \\\\ \\sqrt{558} & =BM\\cdot \\sqrt{27} \\\\ BM & =\\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{186}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645766 :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-3,4,-2⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =2i+4j+5k \\\\  & =⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,u\\times v\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645768 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-1,2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{d}=⟨-1,0,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\times d & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 1)i-((-1)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1)j+((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =6i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,c\\times d\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647782 :question/problem "Let  $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨4,-2⟩.$  Find  $|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653564 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $A(1,e)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,e⟩-⟨1,e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}}⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{2x}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,e) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,\\,\\frac{2(1)}{e}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{2}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇f(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{2}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =2(-1)+\\frac{2}{e}(0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653567 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(1){2}^{1-1}\\,i+({2}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j \\\\  & =i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653588 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7185759e7ed2211fa82e39b3731969a2593645a4da90ce46289b290ff4c32fd.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$ works with the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/30/306fdb7f7a6054d214940d1ca9614c35905f015f34d175561a7850781b751a93.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ea0397e7ba2a2ce6aef1058c6935a3282e12df5adc3b3d564cfb87c5f76c3b.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653594 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =-2x{y}^{3}\\,i-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(2,-1) & =-2(2)(-1)^{3}\\,i-3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}j \\\\  & =4\\,i-12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(2,-1)‖ & =‖4\\,i-12\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656926 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z,\\hspace{0.222em}-xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}3uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-12{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩|}_{x=2{u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=3uv} \\\\  & =⟨-(-v),\\,-3uv,\\,-(2{u}^{2})(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3uv,\\,2{u}^{2}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v,\\,-3uv,\\,2{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩ \\\\  & =v\\cdot 3v+(-3uv)\\cdot (-12{u}^{2})+2{u}^{2}v\\cdot (-4u) \\\\  & =3{v}^{2}+36{u}^{3}v-8{u}^{3}v \\\\  & =3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{v}^{3}+14{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-(-1)+14{u}^{3}-14{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2[u]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656935 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fb6e28df6459782453b94a7516d47407e3625fc13609ab2b85e7e5e2ac597f4.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $y=4$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 16 & =8p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657591 :question/problem "Differentiate ${6}^{x}\\sin(x).$\n\n$(6^{x} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})'=$ $6^{x} \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\,+$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=6^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(x)v(x))' &=u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &=(6^{x})'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(6^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ &=(6^{x} \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(6^{x})(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=6^{x} \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +(6^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657592 :question/problem "Differentiate $-2{e}^{x}\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- 2 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(x)v(x))' &=u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &=(- 2 e^{x})'\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(- 2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ &=(- 2 e^{x})(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(- 2 e^{x})(- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=- 2 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +(2 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657599 :question/problem "Differentiate ${7}^{x}\\sin(x).$\n\n$(7^{x} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})'=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,(7^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=7^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(x)v(x))' &=u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &=(7^{x})'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(7^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ &=(7^{x} \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(7^{x})(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=7^{x} \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +(7^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657600 :question/problem "Differentiate ${2}^{x}\\sin(x).$\n\n$(2^{x} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})'=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,(2^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(x)v(x))' &=u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &=(2^{x})'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(2^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ &=(2^{x} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(2^{x})(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=2^{x} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +(2^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657604 :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(x)v(x))' &=u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &=(x)'\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(x)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ &=(1)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &\\quad +(x)(- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +(- x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657612 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 6\\log_{5} x\\ln x$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=6\\log_{5} x$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(6\\log_{5} x)^{′}\\ln x+6\\log_{5} x(\\ln x)^{′} \\\\  & =(\\frac{6}{x \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}})(\\ln x)+(6\\log_{5} x)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\log_{5} x}{x} + \\frac{6\\ln x}{x\\ln 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657613 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{-2}\\log_{4} x$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x^{-2}$ and $v(x)=\\log_{4} x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(x^{-2})^{′}\\log_{4} x+x^{-2}(\\log_{4} x)^{′} \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2}{x^{3}})(\\log_{4} x)+(x^{-2})(\\frac{1}{x \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}) \\\\  & =x^{-3}(-2\\log_{4} x + \\frac{1}{\\ln 4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657619 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={2}^{x}\\log _{7} (x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{2^{x}}{x \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={2}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\log _{7} (x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({2}^{x})}^{′}\\log _{7} (x)+{2}^{x}{(\\log _{7} (x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({2}^{x}\\ln  (2))(\\log _{7} (x))+({2}^{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\ln  (7)}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\ln  (2)\\log _{7} (x)+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{x\\ln  (7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657623 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\tan(x)\\log(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n**Hint:** $\\log(x)=\\log_{10}(x)$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}}{x \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\tan(x)$ and $v(x)=\\log(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\tan(x))^{′}\\log(x)+\\tan(x)(\\log(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\sec^{2}(x))(\\log(x))+(\\tan(x))(\\frac{1}{x\\ln(10)}) \\\\  & =\\sec^{2}(x)\\log(x)+\\frac{\\tan(x)}{x\\ln(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657627 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=({x}^{3}+4)\\ln(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=3 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=({x}^{3}+4)$ and $v(x)=\\ln(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{3}+4))^{′}\\ln(x)+({x}^{3}+4)(\\ln(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\ln(x))+({x}^{3}+4)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\ln(x)+\\frac{{x}^{3}+4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657674 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+3.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657676 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-6}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-6.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657677 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-2x+12}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2x+12.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-2x+12{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657680 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8x+2}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8x+2.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(8x+2)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657682 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-6-7x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-6-7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-6-7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658970 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2{t}^{2}-2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =xz\\,j+k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k \\\\  & =(-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(4t-2)\\,j+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(4t-2)\\,j+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+(-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (4t-2)+1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{4}+10{t}^{5}-4{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{4}+10{t}^{5}-4{t}^{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{6}-\\frac{4}{7}{t}^{7}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{210}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660367 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y-x$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute $x=\\pi$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & =(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y-x){|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660873 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,2,0),$ $B(3,4,1),$ $C(2,5,3),$ and $D(1,3,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+1k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =2i+2j+1k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+5j+3k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+3j+5k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =j+5k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(3\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 1)-2(1\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 0)+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(15-3)-2(5)+1 \\\\  & =24-10+1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660918 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,2,7⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,1,4⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨5,-2,0⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
